\begin{abstract}
This paper presents the Metric-Wave Continuum Computing Architecture, a 
non-linear field-theoretic hardware platform that performs digital logic 
using wave interference, cubic harmonic generation, and attractor-based 
self-healing dynamics rather than transistor switching. The system is 
governed by a non-linear metric-wave partial differential equation (PDE), 
physically realized on a high-resistivity Silicon-on-Insulator (HR-SOI) 
substrate with VO$_2$ non-linear mixing nodes and ion-implanted absorbing 
boundaries. A 1-bit full adder is implemented using destructive interference 
(Sum) and third-harmonic generation (Carry), validated through simulation 
and mapped to a fabrication-ready GDSII mask stack. An autonomous 
hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) control system using a Zynq UltraScale+ RFSoC 
platform provides phase re-locking, thermal stabilization, and real-time 
noise suppression. The architecture scales to a 64×64 continuum sheet and 
a 16-bit carry-lookahead processor, establishing a new class of 
field-native computing hardware.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Non-linear waves, continuum computing, VO$_2$, harmonic logic, 
Silicon-on-Insulator, RFSoC, autonomous control, field computation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Conventional digital logic relies on transistor switching, clocked 
synchronization, and charge-based state storage. This paper introduces a 
fundamentally different paradigm: computation performed by non-linear 
metric-wave dynamics in a spatial continuum. Digital logic emerges from 
wave interference, cubic non-linear mixing, and attractor-based 
self-healing, enabling computation without transistors, clock trees, or 
charge-based logic states.

The Metric-Wave Continuum Computing Architecture integrates physics, 
device engineering, fabrication, autonomous control, and system-level 
design into a unified computational substrate.

\section{Metric-Wave PDE Formulation}

The scalar metric deviation field $\psi(\bm{x}, t)$ evolves according to:

\begin{equation}
\partial_t \psi =
D_{\text{cont}} \nabla^2 \psi
- K_{\text{local}} \psi
+ \lambda_3 \psi^2
+ \lambda_4 \psi^3
+ \eta \nabla^2 (\partial_t \psi)
+ I_{\text{in}}(\bm{x}, t).
\end{equation}

Parameters:
\begin{align*}
D_{\text{cont}} &= \SI{2.50}{m^2/s}, &
K_{\text{local}} &= \SI{4.18e4}{s^{-1}}, \\
\lambda_3 &= \SI{1.82e6}{s^{-1}}, &
\lambda_4 &= \SI{6.45e7}{s^{-1}}, \\
\eta &= \SI{2.10e3}{s^{-1}}.
\end{align*}

Digital logic is encoded as coherent pulses at carrier frequency 
$f_c = \SI{3.98}{GHz}$.

\section{1-Bit Metric-Wave Full Adder}

\subsection{Sum via Interference}

Inputs A, B, and $C_{\text{in}}$ are routed into a 3 dB directional 
coupler. Odd-parity inputs produce constructive interference:

\[
\psi_{\text{sum}} \ge 0.964 \psi_0,
\]

while even-parity inputs produce destructive interference:

\[
\psi_{\text{sum}} \le 0.043 \psi_0.
\]

\subsection{Carry via Third-Harmonic Generation}

Overlapping pulses at the VO$_2$ non-linear node generate strong 
third-harmonic power:

\[
P_{3\omega} \approx +12.5 \text{ to } +14.2 \text{ dBm}.
\]

Non-overlapping pulses yield:

\[
P_{3\omega} \approx -40 \text{ dBm}.
\]

\section{Fabrication on HR-SOI}

The 1-bit MVP device is fabricated on a high-resistivity SOI wafer:

\begin{itemize}
\item Si waveguide: \SI{220}{nm} thickness, \SI{450}{nm} width.
\item VO$_2$ non-linear film: \SI{50}{nm}.
\item PML boundary: Ar$^+$ implantation at \SI{80}{keV}.
\item Metal pads: Ti/Au (20/150 nm).
\end{itemize}

Six photolithography masks define the full GDSII stack.

\section{Autonomous RFSoC Control Platform}

A Zynq UltraScale+ RFSoC platform provides:

\begin{itemize}
\item Direct-RF DACs at \SI{10}{GSPS} for A, B, $C_{\text{in}}$.
\item Direct-RF ADCs for Sum and Carry channels.
\item Real-time phase recalibration.
\item TEC PID thermal stabilization at $298.15 \pm 0.01$ K.
\item Autonomous BER/SNR monitoring.
\end{itemize}

Critical noise barrier:

\[
\text{SNR}_{\text{crit}} = \SI{10.93}{dB}.
\]

\section{64×64 Continuum Sheet}

The continuum sheet consists of $N = 4096$ nodes arranged in a 
$64 \times 64$ grid. Distributed VO$_2$ nodes enable multi-bit logic, 
shock delocalization, and spatial parallelism.

\section{16-Bit Carry-Lookahead Processor}

Carry-lookahead is implemented using:

\begin{itemize}
\item Parallel harmonic lanes,
\item Phase-matched delay lines,
\item Multi-stage VO$_2$ clusters.
\end{itemize}

Latency target:

\[
\tau_{\text{CLA}} < \SI{60}{ns}.
\]

\section{Metric-Wave Runtime Operating System}

MW-OS provides:

\begin{itemize}
\item Wave packet scheduling,
\item Soliton memory management,
\item Harmonic logic pipelines,
\item Autonomous stability monitoring,
\item Field-level instruction compilation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The Metric-Wave Continuum Computing Architecture demonstrates that 
digital logic can be executed by non-linear wave dynamics, interference, 
and attractor-based stabilization. This establishes a new class of 
field-native computing hardware capable of scaling from 1-bit devices 
to continuum-scale processors.

